# pip's packages live in image/requirements.txt since 2026-09-08, pinned
# by sha256 and installed with --require-hashes. A version alone stops an
# accidental upgrade and stops nothing else. PIP_PINS="" stayed here for
# a while after that, empty, read by nothing: a pin that pins nothing
# reads as a pin.
# The complete list of what the signer carries, with reasons, is the README
# section "What runs on the signer"; tests/test_integrity.py enforces it as
# an import allowlist. Add nothing here without adding it there.
# --- The six packages apt adds, pinned by URL and hash ---------------
#
# MEASURED on the dev board 2026-09-23: `apt-get install -s` for the
# list provision.sh asks for reports exactly SIX packages to install,
# because the 958 the OS image already carries satisfy every
# dependency. So the whole unpinned surface of this card was six files.
#
# PINNED BY URL AND HASH, NOT BY VERSION, and the reason is specific.
# archive.raspberrypi.com/debian is a ROLLING index: it carries only
# the newest version of a package, so apt version pinning cannot reach
# an older one because the version is not in the index to pin to. There
# is no snapshot.raspberrypi.com. deb.debian.org keeps its pool, and
# snapshot.debian.org keeps every version forever, so the Debian four
# stay recoverable after the pool moves on. The Raspberry Pi two do
# not, which is why a release has to carry copies of those files.
#
# Sizes are the archive's own, and they are checked as well as the
# hash: a truncated download that stops at a block boundary is a
# thing that happens, and it fails the size check first with a clearer
# message than a hash mismatch gives.
#
# Format: one package per line, "url sha256 size".
